CI is good at answering one question: did these checks pass on this checkout?

For agent-heavy teams, two harder questions follow. Did the checks run on the
code that will actually merge? And if somebody ran them on a laptop, how can
GitHub tell the difference between evidence and a confident report?

The usual answers each leave a gap. Branch CI runs in a shared environment, but
it usually checks the pull request's head. By merge time, `main` may have moved,
so the candidate that lands can be a different tree. A merge queue closes that
gap by checking the integrated candidate; [our merge-queue post](/blog/merge-queue/)
explains that part in detail.

Running the checks locally is cheaper and often faster. An agent already has
the checkout, dependencies, and tools. But the same agent can choose what to
run and what to report. A green terminal is not an independent witness.

{% mermaid label="Why a green pull request check may cover a different tree from the merge" %}
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["Pull request head"] --> B["Checks pass on<br/>this tree"]
  C["main moves"] --> D["Integrated candidate<br/>may be another tree"]
  B -. "does not prove" .-> D
```
{% /mermaid %}

thwip starts with a narrower question: what exact object does the evidence
cover?

## Sign the tree, not the story

thwip uses the Git tree as the subject of a proof. A tree is the content and
layout of the files at a particular point in Git. Its digest changes when that
snapshot changes. The proof therefore names the thing that was checked, rather
than merely naming a branch or repeating a commit message.

The proof is carried by the commit as a `thwip-proof: v1...` Git trailer: a
signed DSSE envelope around an in-toto statement. In simplified form, its
important fields look like this:

```json
{
  "subject": [{ "name": "git-tree", "digest": { "sha1": "…" } }],
  "predicate": {
    "git": {
      "treeOid": "…",
      "baseSha": "…",
      "evaluationMode": "head-tree"
    },
    "run": {
      "suites": [{ "id": "test", "status": "passed" }]
    }
  }
}
```

The real predicate also names the base, policy, pipeline, runner, suite
durations, and signing key. The useful invariant is simple: a proof for one
tree cannot satisfy a check for another. If the tree GitHub is asked to merge
does not match the proof, the required check fails with a subject mismatch.

{% mermaid label="How a signed proof becomes a GitHub merge check" %}
```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A["Run required suites on<br/>one exact Git tree"] --> B["Build proof with<br/>tree, results, and policy"]
  B --> C["Sign with enrolled<br/>device key"]
  C --> D["GitHub App verifies<br/>signature and claim"]
  D --> E{"Does the tree and policy match?"}
  E -- "no" --> F["Required check fails"]
  E -- "yes" --> G["Required check passes"]
  G --> H["Queue or GitHub may merge"]
```
{% /mermaid %}

This is what the signature establishes:

- The envelope was signed by a particular enrolled device key.
- The signed bytes have not changed.
- The signer claimed that named suites passed on the named tree under the
  named policy and pipeline.
- GitHub can check the key's enrollment and repository permission, then compare
  the proof's tree and base with the change under review.

The signature does not establish that the suites really ran. It does not make
the machine honest, observe its network or cache, or detect a compromised
device. A person holding the private key can sign a false but well-formed
claim. That is the central trade-off: thwip makes the claim precise and
checkable, but it does not turn a local machine into a remote witness.

The thwip GitHub App performs this verification without running repository code.
It checks the signature, signer, policy and pipeline digests, suite results, and
the exact subject tree. It then owns the required `thwip` check that branch
protection can require. The App is verifying a claim about a tree; it is not
pretending to have watched the original process.

## Four ways to pay for confidence

thwip makes the missing witness explicit with four profiles. They are choices
about what a merge is allowed to trust and where the compute happens.

| Profile | Evidence for the merge | Main trade-off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fast` | Reuse a signed proof on the pull request head. | Cheapest and fastest; the integrated candidate is not run again. |
| `fresh` | A device runs the pinned integrated candidate and signs a receipt naming its tree. | No cloud rerun; you trust the runner and need it available. |
| `remote` | A pinned GitHub Actions verifier runs the candidate. | GitHub is the shared witness; every candidate costs CI time. |
| `audited` | Run on a device, then rerun the required suites in GitHub Actions. | Strongest check, with both execution costs. |

`fast` is deliberately honest about what it proves: the head proof is valid,
not that the post-merge candidate was executed. For `fresh`, the queue signs an
assignment naming the exact candidate tree and required suites. The device
checks that assignment, runs those suites, and signs a receipt. The control
plane accepts the receipt only when it answers that assignment and names the
same tree. `remote` moves the witness to GitHub. `audited` asks for both.

The cost difference is practical, not theoretical. In 18 successful candidate
verifications in this repository, Actions spent a median 36 seconds on runner
setup, checkout, dependency installation, and workspace builds before a suite
started. The already-warm local suite results had a median of 4.5 seconds.
Those numbers belong to this repository, not every project, but they show why a
team may choose to spend trust in a device rather than spend another cloud run.

GitHub also bills runner time by runner class and rounds jobs up to whole
minutes. A remote verifier buys a familiar, shared execution environment. A
fresh receipt buys lower cloud cost and a run against the integrated tree, but
it relies on a project runner and a signing key. The right profile depends on
which of those costs your team can accept. [GitHub's published rates](https://docs.github.com/en/billing/reference/actions-minute-multipliers)
make the cloud side visible; thwip makes the trust side visible too.

## What this does not solve

Proofs are not a substitute for review. A passing suite says that named checks
passed on a named tree. It says nothing about whether the product is secure or
whether the tests cover the important behavior.

They also do not remove environment differences. If a suite needs a cloud
service, a platform-specific runner, or a secret that cannot exist on a local
machine, keep that suite remote. A project can combine remote suites with a
local profile; `audited` adds a full GitHub rerun when the stronger reference is
worth its cost.

The hardest remaining case is a compromised signing device under `fast`. With
no second execution, there is no mechanism that can detect the first false
proof. `fresh` narrows the claim by requiring a receipt for the integrated
candidate; `remote` and `audited` add GitHub's independent execution. None of
them makes a stolen private key harmless.

That boundary is the point of the model. A proof is valuable because it names
exactly what was claimed, who signed it, and what GitHub checked. It is not
valuable because the word “passed” appeared in a terminal.

